ph $EI({\\cal H})=(V,{\\cal E}^{EI})$ has the edge set ${\\cal E}^{EI}=\\{e_1 \\cap e_2 \\ |\\ e_1, e_2 \\in {\\cal E} \\ \\wedge \\ e_1 \\neq e_2 \\ \\wedge \\ |e_1 \\cap e_2 |\\geq2\\}$. Picking up a problem from arXiv:1901.06292, for $n \\ge 24$ we prove that there is a 3-regular (and - if $n$ is even - 6-uniform) hypergraph ${\\cal H}=(V,{\\cal E})$ with $\\lceil \\frac{n}{2} \\rceil$ hyperedges and $EI({\\cal H}) = C_n$.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nHigh-girth approximation conjecture for uniquely restricted matching number | MathDB (https://mathdb.com/p/338806/high-girth-approximation-conjecture-for-uniquely-restricted)\nciteturn0search0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 months ago; High-girth approximation conjecture for uniquely restricted matching number\n\nHigh-girth approximation conjecture for uniquely restricted matching number\n\nAbout 8 years old · traced to 2018\n\nFor a graph $G$G, let $\\nu(G)$ν(G) denote its matching number and $\\nu_{ur}(G)$ν u r​(G) its uniquely restricted matching number. For every positive real number $\\epsilon$ϵ and every positive integer $\\Delta$Δ, consider graphs of maximum degree at most $\\Delta$Δ and girth at least $g$g. High-girth approximation conjecture. There is some positive integer $g=g(\\epsilon,\\Delta)$g=g(ϵ,Δ) such that\n\n$$\\nu_{ur}(G)\\geq (1-\\epsilon)\\nu(G)$$ν u r​(G)≥(1−ϵ)ν(G)\n\nfor every graph $G$G of maximum degree at most $\\Delta$Δ and girth at least $g$g. The conjecture asserts that, for fixed maximum degree, sufficiently large girth makes the uniquely restricted matching number asymptotically as close as desired to the ordinary matching number; the source gives no resolution, so it remains open.\n\n## Primary source\n\nM. Fürst and D. Rautenbach, “Some bounds on the uniquely restricted matching number”, arXiv:1803.11032 (2018).\n\ngraph-theoryhigh-girth-graphsmatchings\n\n0 0\n\n3 weeks ago\n\n## Progress summary\n\nRefresh Never refreshed\n\nNothing recorded yet. Refresh searches the literature and the public web for attempts on this problem, and writes the first summary here.\n\n## Solutions 0--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCycles as edge intersection hypergraphs of $k$-uniform hypergraphs ($k \\le 6$) -- a constructive approach (https://arxiv.org/abs/2211.06245)\nciteturn0academia14 [wordlim: 200] Published: 4.0 years ago; Title: Cycles as edge intersection hypergraphs of $k$-uniform hypergraphs ($k \\le 6$) -- a constructive approach\nTitle: Cycles as edge intersection hypergraphs of $k$-uniform hypergraphs ($k \\le 6$) -- a constructive approach\nAuthors: Sophie Pätz, Martin Sonntag\nDate: Fri Nov 11 14:36:37 2022\n\nIf ${\\cal H}=(V,{\\cal E})$ is a hypergraph, its edge intersection hypergraph $EI({\\cal H})=(V,{\\cal E}^{EI})$ has the edge set ${\\cal E}^{EI}=\\{e_1 \\cap e_2 \\ |\\ e_1, e_2 \\in {\\cal E} \\ \\wedge \\ e_1 \\neq e_2 \\ \\wedge \\ |e_1 \\cap e_2 |\\geq 2\\}$. In the present paper, we consider 4- and 5-uniform hypergraphs ${\\cal H}$, respectively, with $EI({\\cal H}) = C_n$. Our results fill the gap between the 3- and the 6-uniform case considered in arXiv:1902.00396.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCycles as edge intersection hypergraphs (https://research.uni-luebeck.de/en/publications/cycles-as-edge-intersection-hypergraphs/)\nciteturn0search1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 6 days ago; # Cycles as edge intersection hypergraphs ... Pickingupaproblem from [7], for n ≥ 24 we prove that there is a 3-regular (and - if n is even - 6-uniform) hypergraph H = (V,E) with ⌈n⌉ hyperedges and EI(H) = Cn.\n\n# Cycles as edge intersection hypergraphs\n\n  * Martin Sonntag\n  * , Hanns-Martin Teichert\n\n  * Institute of Mathematics\n\n  * Technische Universität Bergakademie Freiberg\n\n## Abstract\n\nIf H = (V,E) is a hypergraph, its edge intersection hypergraph EI(H) = (V,EEI) hastheedgesetEEI={e1∩e2|e1,e2∈E∧e1̸=e2 ∧|e1∩e2|≥2}.Pickingupaproblem from [7], for n ≥ 24 we prove that there is a 3-regular (and - if n is even - 6-uniform) hypergraph H = (V,E) with ⌈n⌉ hyperedges and EI(H) = Cn.\n\nOriginal language  | English\nPublication status  | Published - 2019\n\n## Access to Document\n\n  * https://arxiv.org/abs/1902.00396\n\nSonntag, M., & Teichert, H.-M. (2019). Cycles as edge intersection hypergraphs. https://arxiv.org/abs/1902.00396\n\nSonntag, Martin ; Teichert, Hanns-Martin. / Cycles as edge intersection hypergraphs. 2019. 21 p.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nUniquely restricted matchings in subcubic graphs (https://arxiv.org/abs/1805.00840)\nciteturn0academia15 [wordlim: 200] Published: 8.5 years ago; Moreover, we prove that every connected subcubic graph of order $n$ and girth at least $7$ has a uniquely restricted matching of size at least $\\frac{n-1}{3}$, which partially confirms a Conjecture of F\\\"{u}rst and Rautenbach (Some bounds on the uniquely restricted matching number, arXiv:1803.11032).\nTitle: Uniquely restricted matchings in subcubic graphs\nAuthors: Maximilian Fürst, Michael A. Henning, Dieter Rautenbach\nDate: Wed May  2 14:20:04 2018\n\nA matching $M$ in a graph $G$ is uniquely restricted if no other matching in $G$ covers the same set of vertices. We conjecture that every connected subcubic graph with $m$ edges and $b$ bridges that is distinct from $K_{3,3}$ has a uniquely restricted matching of size at least $\\frac{m+b}{6}$, and we establish this bound with $b$ replaced by the number of bridges that lie on a path between two vertices of degree at most $2$. Moreover, we prove that every connected subcubic graph of order $n$ and girth at least $7$ has a uniquely restricted matching of size at least $\\frac{n-1}{3}$, which partially confirms a Conjecture of F\\\"{u}rst and Rautenbach (Some bounds on the uniquely restricted matching number, arXiv:1803.11032).---------------------------