ubic graphs. / Fürst, Maximilian; Henning, Michael A.; Rautenbach, Dieter.\nIn: Discrete Applied Mathematics, Vol. 262, 15.06.2019, p. 189-194.\n\nResearch output: Contribution to journal › Article › peer-review\n\nTY - JOUR\n\nT1 - Uniquely restricted matchings in subcubic graphs\n\nAU - Fürst, Maximilian\n\nAU - Henning, Michael A.\n\nAU - Rautenbach, Dieter\n\nN1 - Publisher Copyright: © 2019 Elsevier B.V.\n\nPY - 2019/6/15\n\nY1 - 2019/6/15\n\nN2 - A matching M in a graph G is uniquely restricted if no other matching in G covers the same set of vertices. We conjecture that every connected subcubic graph with m edges and b bridges that is distinct from K 3,3 has a uniquely restricted matching of size at least m/+b6, and we establish this bound with b replaced by the number of bridges that lie on a path between two vertices of degree at most 2. Moreover, we prove that every connected subcubic graph of order n and girth at least 7 has a uniquely restricted matching of size at least n/−13, which partially confirms a conjecture of Fürst and Rautenbach (Graphs Combin. 35 (2019) 353–361).\n\nAB - A matching M in a graph G is uniquely restricted if no other matching in G covers the same set of vertices. We conjecture that every connected subcubic graph with m edges and b bridges that is distinct from K 3,3 has a uniquely restricted matching of size at least m/+b6, and we establish this bound with b replaced by the number of bridges that lie on a path between two vertices of degree at most 2. Moreover, we prove that every connected subcubic graph of order n and girth at least 7 has a uniquely restricted matching of size at least n/−13, which partially confirms a conjecture of Fürst and Rautenbach (Graphs Combin. 35 (2019) 353–361).\n\nJO - Discrete Applied Mathematics\n\nJF - Discrete Applied Mathematics\n\nER -\n\nFürst M, Henning MA, Rautenbach D. Uniquely restricted matchings in subcubic graphs. Discrete Applied Mathematics. 2019 Jun 15;262:189-194. doi: 10.1016/j.dam.2019.02.013\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nUniquely restricted matchings in subcubic graphs without short cycles (https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/full/10.1002/jgt.22632)\nciteturn0search3 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6.1 years ago; Crawled: last month; # Uniquely restricted matchings in subcubic graphs without short cycles ... We prove that any connected subcubic graph with 𝑛n vertices and girth at least 5 contains a uniquely restricted matching of size at least (𝑛−1)/3\\left(n-1\\right)/3 except for two exceptional cubic graphs of order 14 and 20. ... Plummer, Matching theory, North-Holland, Amsterdam, 1986.\n\nCorrespondence D. Rautenbach, Institute of Optimization and Operations Research, Ulm University, Ulm, Germany.\n\nEmail: dieter.rautenbach@uni-ulm.de\n\nFirst published: 19 October 2020\n\nhttps://doi.org/10.1002/jgt.22632Digital Object Identifier (DOI)\n\nview metrics\n\nRead the full text\n\n  * References\n\n### Abstract\n\nA matching 𝑀M in a graph 𝐺G is uniquely restricted if no other matching in 𝐺G covers the same set of vertices. We prove that any connected subcubic graph with 𝑛n vertices and girth at least 5 contains a uniquely restricted matching of size at least (𝑛−1)/3\\left(n-1\\right)/3 except for two exceptional cubic graphs of order 14 and 20.\n\nREFERENCES\n\n  * 1 V. Costa, S. Dantas, and D. Rautenbach, Matchings in graphs of odd regularity and girth, Discrete Math. 313 (2013), 2895–2902.\n\nWeb of Science® Google Scholar\n\n  * 2 A. D. Flaxman and S. Hoory, Maximum matchings in regular graphs of high girth, Electron. J. Combinatorics 14 (2007), #N1.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nUniquely restricted matchings in subcubic graphs - arXiv Combinatorics (https://www.arxiv.symmetricfunctions.com/paper/1805.00840v1)\nciteturn0search4 [wordlim: 200] Published: 8.5 years ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; Moreover, we prove that every connected subcubic graph of order $n$ and girth at least $7$ has a uniquely restricted matching of size at least $\\frac{n-1}{3}$, which partially confirms a Conjecture of Fürst and Rautenbach (Some bounds on the uniquely restricted matching number, arXiv:1803.11032).\n\n## Uniquely restricted matchings in subcubic graphs\n\nMaximilian Fürst, Michael A. Henning, Dieter Rautenbach\n\nPublished: 2018-05-02\n\n\ngraph matching\n\n# • BIB • PDF • ARXIV •\n\n### Abstract\n\nA matching $M$ in a graph $G$ is uniquely restricted if no other matching in $G$ covers the same set of vertices. We conjecture that every connected subcubic graph with $m$ edges and $b$ bridges that is distinct from $K_{3,3}$ has a uniquely restricted matching of size at least $\\frac{m+b}{6}$, and we establish this bound with $b$ replaced by the number of bridges that lie on a path between two vertices of degree at most $2$. Moreover, we prove that every connected subcubic graph of order $n$ and girth at least $7$ has a uniquely restricted matching of size at least $\\frac{n-1}{3}$, which partially confirms a Conjecture of Fürst and Rautenbach (Some bounds on the uniquely restricted matching number, arXiv:1803.11032).\n\n### BibTeX\n\n[Button: arXiv BibTeX ] [Button: Published BibTeX ]--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nUniquely restricted matchings in subcubic graphs without short cycles (https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.1002/jgt.22632)\nciteturn0search5 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6.1 years ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; A matching M in a graph G is uniquely restricted if no other matching in G covers the same set of vertices. We prove that any connected subcubic graph with n vertices and girth at least 5 contains a uniquely restricted matching of size at least \\left(n-1\\right)/3 except for two exceptional cubic graphs of order 14 and 20.\n\nVolume 96, Issue 4 pp. 578-593\n\nARTICLE\n\n# Uniquely restricted matchings in subcubic graphs without short cycles\n\nM. Fürst, \n\nM. Fürst\n\nInstitute of Optimization and Operations Research, Ulm University, Ulm, Germany\n\nSearch for more papers by this author\n\nD. Rautenbach, \n\nCorresponding Author\n\nD. Rautenbach\n\n  * dieter.rautenbach@uni-ulm.de\n\n  * orcid.org/0000-0002-7214-042X\n\nInstitute of Optimization and Operations Research, Ulm University, Ulm, Germany\n\nCorrespondence D. Rautenbach, Institute of Optimization and Operations Research, Ulm University, Ulm, Germany.\n\nEmail: dieter.rautenbach@uni-ulm.de\n\nSearch for more papers by this author\n\nEmail: dieter.rautenbach@uni-ulm.de\n\nSearch for more papers by this author\n\nFirst published: 19 October 2020\n\nhttps://doi.org/10.1002/jgt.22632Digital Object Identifier (DOI)\n\nview metrics\n\n### Abstract\n\nA matching M in a graph G is uniquely restricted if no other matching in G covers the same set of vertices. We prove that any connected subcubic graph with n vertices and girth at least 5 contains a uniquely restricted matching of size at least \\left(n-1\\right)/3 except for two exceptional cubic graphs of order 14 and 20.\n\n## \n\nVolume96, Issue4\n\nMarch 2021\n\nPages 578-593\n\n\nFürst M, Rautenbach D. Uniquely restricted matchings in subcubic graphs without short cycles. J Graph Theory. 2021; 96: 578–593. https://doi.org/10.1002/jgt.22632\n\nCOPY TEXT\n\n* * *\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n2-Connected Subgraphs of All Orders in Large Graphs with Minimum Degree at Least n/q (https://papers.cool/arxiv/2607.18964)\nciteturn0search6 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3 months ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; # 2607.18964 ... Second, an averaging argument on common neighborhoods yields a large complete bipartite subgraph $K_{2,t}$, which provides $2$-connected subgraphs of all the remaining orders.Finally, we propose the following conjecture: for every pair of fixed integers $r\\ge 2$ and $q\\ge 3$, every $r$-connected graph $G$ of sufficiently large order $n$ with $δ(G)\\ge n/q$ contains an $r$-connected subgraph of every order from $2r$ to $n$.\n# 2607.18964\n\nTotal: 1\n\n## #1 2-Connected Subgraphs of All Orders in Large Graphs with Minimum Degree at Least n/q [PDF^{}] [Copy] [Kimi^{}] [REL]\n\nAuthor: Heng Yang\n\nWe confirm a conjecture of Liu and Ning\\cite{LiuNing}: for every fixed integer $q\\ge 3$, there exists an integer $n_0(q)$ such that every $2$-connected graph $G$ of order $n\\ge n_0(q)$ with minimum degree $δ(G)\\ge n/q$ contains a $2$-connected subgraph of order $\\ell$ for each $\\ell\\in\\{4,5,\\ldots,n\\}$. The proof has two main components. First, we construct a small $2$-connected subgraph $D$ such that every vertex outside $D$ has at least two neighbors in $D$. By successively adding short paths, we obtain $2$-connected subgraphs of every order from $|V(D)|$ to $n$. Second, an averaging argument on common neighborhoods yields a large complete bipartite subgraph $K_{2,t}$, which provides $2$-connected subgraphs of all the remaining orders.Finally, we propose the following conjecture: for every pair of fixed integers $r\\ge 2$ and $q\\ge 3$, every $r$-connected graph $G$ of sufficiently large order $n$ with $δ(G)\\ge n/q$ contains an $r$-connected subgraph of every order from $2r$ to $n$.\n\nSubject: Combinatorics\n\nPublish: 2026-07-21 10:55:13 UTC--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nUniquely restricted matchings in subcubic graphs without short cycles (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/345546239_Uniquely_restricted_matchings_in_subcubic_graphs_without_short_cycles)\nciteturn0search7 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6.1 years ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; We prove that any connected subcubic graph with n vertices and girth at least 5 contains a uniquely restricted matching of size at least (n−1)/3 except for two exceptional cubic graphs of order 14 and 20. ... girth, matching, subcubic, uniquely restricted matching\nNote that the dashed edges belong to the graph… 】\n\n+3\n\nJ Graph Theory. 2021;96:578–593.wileyonlinelibrary.com/journal/jgt 578\n\n|\n\n© 2020 Wiley Periodicals LLC\n\nReceived: 10 October 2018\n\n|\n\nRevised: 14 September 2020\n\n|\n\nAccepted: 30 September 2020\n\nDOI: 10.1002/jgt.22632\n\nUniquely restricted matchings in subcubic\n\ngraphs without short cycles\n\nM. Fürst |D. Rautenbach\n\nInstitute of Optimization and Operations\n\nResearch, Ulm University, Ulm, Germany\n\nCorrespondence\n\nD. Rautenbach, Institute of Optimization\n\nand Operations Research, Ulm University,\n\nUlm, Germany.\n\nEmail: dieter.rautenbach@uni-ulm.de\n\nAbstract\n\nA matching\n\nM\n\nin a graph\n\nG\n\nis uniquely restricted if\n\nno other matching in\n\nG\n\ncovers the same set of ver-\n\ntices. We prove that any connected subcubic graph\n\nwith n vertices and girth at least 5 contains a uniquely\n\nrestricted matching of size at least\n\nn\n\n(\n\n−1)/3\n\nexcept\n\nfor two exceptional cubic graphs of order 14 and 20.\n\nKEYWORDS\n\ngirth, matching, subcubic, uniquely restricted matching\n\n05C70\n\n1|INTRODUCTION\n\nWe consider simple, finite, and undirected graphs, and use standard terminology. A matching\n\nM\n\nin a graph\n\nG\n\nis uniquely restricted [5] if no other matching in\n\nG\n\ncovers the same set of\n\nvertices. The maximum cardinalities of a matching and a uniquely restricted matching in\n\nG\n\n,\n\ncalled the matching number and the uniquely restricted matching number of\n\nG\n\n, will be denoted\n\nby\n\nν G(\n\n)\n\nand\n\nν G(\n\n)\n\nur\n\n, respectively. While determining the matching number is tractable [9],\n\ndetermining the uniquely restricted matching number is NP‐hard [5]. In the present paper, we\n\nestablish a tight lower bound on the uniquely restricted matching number in subcubic graphs of\n\ngirth at least 5. The search for bounds in graphs of bounded degree and large girth is a common\n\ntask for various hard graph invariants, cf. [1,2,6,7,10,11]. In [4] we proved that\n\nν G