f Fürst and Rautenbach (Graphs Combin. 35 (2019) 353–361).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nUniquely restricted matchings in subcubic graphs (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/324908434_Uniquely_restricted_matchings_in_subcubic_graphs)\nciteturn0search9 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 months ago; Moreover, we prove that every connected subcubic graph of order n and girth at least 7 has a uniquely restricted matching of size at least $\\frac{n-1}{3}$, which partially confirms a Conjecture of F\\\"{u}rst and Rautenbach (Some bounds on the uniquely restricted matching number, arXiv:1803.11032). ... In this paper we introduce a new, specialized type of matching which we call uniquely restricted matchings, originally motivated by the problem of determining a lower bound on the rank of a matrix having a specified zero/ non-zero pattern.\nPreprint\n\n# Uniquely restricted matchings in subcubic graphs\n\n  * May 2018\n\nDOI:10.48550/arXiv.1805.00840\n\nAuthors:\n\nMaximilian Fuerst\n\n  * Ulm University\n\nMichael A. Henning\n\n  * University of Johannesburg\n\nDieter Rautenbach\n\nDieter Rautenbach\n\n  * This person is not on ResearchGate, or hasn't claimed this research yet.\n\nImage: Request Full-text Paper PDF\n\nRequest file PDF\n\nTo read the file of this research, you can request a copy directly from the authors.\n\n## Abstract\n\nA matching M in a graph G is uniquely restricted if no other matching in G covers the same set of vertices. We conjecture that every connected subcubic graph with m edges and b bridges that is distinct from $K_{3,3}$ has a uniquely restricted matching of size at least $\\frac{m+b}{6}$, and we establish this bound with b replaced by the number of bridges that lie on a path between two vertices of degree at most 2. Moreover, we prove that every connected subcubic graph of order n and girth at least 7 has a uniquely restricted matching of size at least $\\frac{n-1}{3}$, which partially confirms a Conjecture of F\\\"{u}rst and Rautenbach (Some bounds on the uniquely restricted matching number, arXiv:1803.11032).\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nMaximilian Fürst - arXiv Combinatorics (https://www.arxiv.symmetricfunctions.com/author/maximilian-furst)\nciteturn0search10 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 months ago; For the unweighted case though, we conjecture that the integrality gap is at most $\\frac{5}{8}Δ+O(1)$, and that also the approximation ratio can be improved at least to this value. ... We prove that any connected subcubic graph with $n$ vertices and girth at least $5$ contains a uniquely restricted matching of size at least $(n-1) / 3$ except for two exceptional cubic graphs of order $14$ and $20$.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCycles as edge intersection hypergraphs (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/330844999_Cycles_as_edge_intersection_hypergraphs)\nciteturn0search11 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 5 months ago; # Cycles as edge intersection hypergraphs ... In this paper it is proved that there is a finite family F of graphs such that any graph G with minimum degree at least 69 is the intersection graph of a 3-uniform linear hypergraph if and only if G has no induced subgraph isomorphic to a member of F. ... It is shown that the strong perfect graph conjecture holds for EPT graphs. ... The class of EPT graphs is equivalent to the class of fundamental cycle graphs.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nUniquely restricted matchings in subcubic graphs without short cycles (https://arxiv.org/abs/1810.04473)\nciteturn0academia16 [wordlim: 200] Published: 8.1 years ago; We prove that any connected subcubic graph with $n$ vertices and girth at least $5$ contains a uniquely restricted matching of size at least $(n-1) / 3$ except for two exceptional cubic graphs of order $14$ and $20$.\n"